From: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Subject: mm/highmem: reject previously poisoned pages in machine-check copies
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 23:21:22 -0800

A page already marked PG_hwpoison need not raise another machine check
when it is read.  On architectures with copy_mc_to_kernel(),
copy_mc_user_highpage() and copy_mc_highpage() can consequently return
success after copying it.  Fork, COW and migration can then install a new
page containing data from a known-poisoned source, without marking the new
page poisoned.  If the copy implementation cannot recover from a physical
error, consuming that source can instead crash the kernel.  This requires
an existing poisoned page: ordinary userspace cannot inject poison, but a
task can encounter a hardware-poisoned page.

Yuanhe Shu's change [1] rejects previously poisoned pages in the
!copy_mc_to_kernel fallbacks.  Apply the same check to the
copy_mc_to_kernel implementations.  Return PAGE_SIZE, their existing
not-copied-byte convention, without queueing another memory failure for a
page that is already marked poisoned.

Also check the complete source folio at the start of folio_mc_copy(). 
That catches a poisoned subpage before migration copies any of the folio,
including when Shivank Garg's proposed batched copy [2] bypasses the
per-page helper.  These checks reject poison already recorded when copying
starts; a concurrent poison event can still race with the check, while
synchronous machine checks remain handled by the copy operation.

